.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt NOQTIS-PASSWD 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm noqtis-passwd
.Nd change or remove the passphrase of a secret key
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm noqtis passwd
.Fl k Ar key
.Op Fl -pass-fd Ar n | Fl -pass-env Ar name
.Fl -new-pass-fd Ar n | Fl -new-pass-env Ar name | Fl -no-pass
.Op Fl -kdf Cm argon2id | pbkdf2
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm noqtis passwd
reads the secret key file
.Ar key
and writes it again under another passphrase, or under none.
A signature secret key of version 1
.Pq Ql noqtis-key-1 <alg> sec ,
written before noqtis 0.3.0, is written again as version 2, with the
integrity value that signing checks
.Pq Xr nqsxmk 3 ;
this is the way to add one to an old key, with
.Fl -no-pass
for a key under no passphrase.
The key itself does not change: its public key
.Pq Ar key Ns Pa .pub ,
every signature made with it and everything sealed to it stay as they
are.
The file is written under a temporary name beside
.Ar key
and renamed over it once whole, so a failure leaves the old file.
It prints one line naming the file, the algorithm and what the key is
under now: no passphrase, or the new one and the KDF that protects the
file
.Pq Argon2id or PBKDF2 .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl k Ar key , Fl -key Ns = Ns Ar key
The secret key file, as
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1
wrote it.
.It Fl -pass-fd Ar n , Fl -pass-env Ar name
The passphrase
.Ar key
is under now, read as
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1
reads one; neither is given for a key under no passphrase.
.It Fl -new-pass-fd Ar n , Fl -new-pass-env Ar name
The new passphrase, from the open file descriptor
.Ar n
(up to a newline or the end) or from the environment variable called
.Ar name .
It is at most 4096 bytes and never empty, and is never given on the
command line, echoed or printed.
.Fl -pass-fd
and
.Fl -new-pass-fd
must name two descriptors.
.It Fl -no-pass
Keep the key under no passphrase.
.It Fl -kdf Cm argon2id | pbkdf2
How the new passphrase becomes the key that protects the secret key, as
for
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1 :
.Cm argon2id
(the default) or
.Cm pbkdf2
(PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA-256, 600000 iterations).
Without it the new file keeps the KDF the old one was under, so a key
under PBKDF2 stays under PBKDF2; a key under no passphrase gets
.Cm argon2id .
Not with
.Fl -no-pass .
The old file is read whichever it was made with.
.El
.Pp
One of
.Fl -new-pass-fd ,
.Fl -new-pass-env
and
.Fl -no-pass
is required.
The new file is protected as
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1
protects one: ChaCha20-Poly1305 with a key from Argon2id of the
passphrase (64 MiB, 3 passes, 4 lanes) or from PBKDF2, as above, and a
new random salt
.Pq Xr nqpwenc 3 .
A copy of the old file, a backup for one, still opens with the old
passphrase: changing the passphrase does not undo a passphrase that
leaked together with the file.
For that, make a new key and say so with
.Xr noqtis-rotate 1 .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 when the key was written; 2 on a usage or file error, a wrong
passphrase among them.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal
$ noqtis passwd -k me --pass-fd 3 --new-pass-fd 4 3<old.txt 4<new.txt
me: ML-DSA-65 secret key, now under the new passphrase (Argon2id)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-keygen 1 ,
.Xr noqtis-rotate 1 ,
.Xr nqpwenc 3 ,
.Xr nqsxmk 3
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 9106 (Argon2id), RFC 8018 (PBKDF2), RFC 8439 (ChaCha20-Poly1305).
